.Dd October 27, 2026
.Dt PALETA 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm Paleta
.Nd the desktop's palette, and the root window property that carries it
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
import Paleta;      (* installed in ~/.raz/lib *)

const
  Atom = "_TINTA";  Items = 22;  Version = 1;

  Black = 0; Red = 1; Green = 2; Yellow = 3; Blue = 4;
  Magenta = 5; Cyan = 6; White = 7;   (* + Bright *)
  Bright = 8;
  Fg = 16; Bg = 17; Cursor = 18; Mark = 19;
  Slots = 20;  Themes = 4;

  Short, Unknown, BadMode: ERROR;     (* decode's errors *)

type
  Mode    = (dark, light);
  Theme   = (catppuccin, duck, cyberpunk, phosphor);
  Palette = record
    mode: Mode;
    col: [Slots]U32;                  (* 0xRRGGBB *)
  end;

const
  Dark, Light, DuckDark, DuckLight: Palette;
  CyberDark, CyberLight, PhosphorDark, PhosphorLight: Palette;

proc themePalette(th: Theme; m: Mode): Palette;
proc themeOf(const p: Palette): (Theme, BOOL);
proc themeName(th: Theme): []const U8;
proc encode(const p: Palette): [Items]U32;
proc decode(data: []const U32; var p: Palette): ERROR;
.Ed
.Pp
Compile a program that imports it as usual:
.Ic robbie install
in
.Pa ~/r/tinta
puts a plain copy in
.Pa ~/.raz/lib ,
where raz finds it.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
holds the palettes of
.Xr tinta 1
and turns one into the numbers of the
.Li _TINTA
property and back.
It makes no X calls, so a program reads the property with whatever
binding it already has and hands the items here.
It imports nothing but the library's
.Sy Math ,
.Sy Fmt
and
.Sy Rt ,
and its own
.Ic main
is its test.
.Pp
A palette is 20 colours, each
.Li 0xRRGGBB ,
in slots: 0 to 7 the ANSI colours black, red, green, yellow, blue,
magenta, cyan and white, 8 to 15 the same bright (add
.Dv Bright ) ,
.Dv Fg
the foreground,
.Dv Bg
the background,
.Dv Cursor
the cursor, and
.Dv Mark
an accent, which
.Xr rat 1
and
.Xr pt 1
draw their tab bar and selections in.
With the colours goes a mode, for programs that keep colours of their
own and only need to know which kind to use.
.Pp
.Dv Dark
is Catppuccin Mocha's terminal palette, with its peach as the mark
and bright black, the colour of dim text, lightened, keeping its hue,
to a contrast ratio of 4.5:1 against the background.
.Dv Light
is Latte's, with every colour but black and red darkened, keeping
its hue, until each has a contrast ratio of 4.5:1 against the
background, what WCAG asks of ordinary text; the foreground has 7:1.
.Pp
Two palettes, one dark and one light, make a theme.
.Dv Dark
and
.Dv Light
are the theme
.Dv catppuccin ;
.Dv DuckDark
and
.Dv DuckLight
are
.Dv duck ,
the duck theme of
.Sy ladle :
a mallard's green and its bill's orange, on a pale green-grey by day and a
pond's deep teal by night.
Duck names roles, not ANSI colours, so each slot takes the nearest
role (strings green, keywords cyan, types yellow, numbers blue),
and a red in its tone is added; its colours read at 6:1 or better.
Cyberpunk, the pair
.Dv CyberDark
and
.Dv CyberLight ,
is a city's neon:
.Dv CyberDark
has hot pink above all, with fluorescent cyan and acid green, on a
violet-black sky, and
.Dv CyberLight
deep violet text on hot-pink-tinted paper, with electric indigo for blue,
teal for green, lilac whites and a hot pink cursor and mark.
Phosphor, the pair
.Dv PhosphorDark
and
.Dv PhosphorLight ,
is an old terminal:
.Dv PhosphorDark
is P1 green on black glass: pale phosphor text, so that the full green
and a true red, a diff's added and removed lines, stand out from it,
with amber for the mark and the whites leaning green; and
.Dv PhosphorLight
near-black green ink on green-barred fanfold paper, with a clear green
and a brick red, every colour leaning green (teal for blue, green-greys
for the whites), with an amber mark.
Every text colour of every palette has a contrast ratio of 4.5:1 or
better, and the foreground 7:1 or better; the test checks it.
The property carries colours, not a theme, so
.Fn themeOf
tells the theme from them.
.Pp
The property is
.Dv Atom
on the root window, type
.Li CARDINAL
(atom 6), format 32,
.Dv Items
items: the version, the mode (0 dark, 1 light), then the 20 slots in
order.
When it is absent, use
.Dv Dark .
.Ss Routines
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn themePalette th m
The palette of theme
.Fa th
in mode
.Fa m .
.It Fn themeOf p
The theme whose palette for
.Fa p Ns 's
mode has exactly
.Fa p Ns 's
colours, and true; or false when there is none.
.It Fn themeName th
The theme's name in lower case:
one of
.Li catppuccin ,
.Li duck ,
.Li cyberpunk
or
.Li phosphor .
.It Fn encode p
The
.Dv Items
numbers of
.Fa p ,
ready for
.Fn XChangeProperty .
.It Fn decode data p
Reads
.Fa data ,
as the property returned it, into
.Fa p ,
and returns nil, or an error.
Items beyond
.Dv Items
and the top byte of each colour are ignored.
On an error
.Fa p
is left as it was, so a caller that set it to
.Dv Dark
first can use it either way.
.El
.Ss Following the palette
Read
.Li _TINTA
once at start; select
.Dv PropertyChangeMask
on the root; on each
.Dv PropertyNotify
whose atom is
.Li _TINTA ,
read it again and redraw.
A deleted property is the dark palette.
A program chooses how closely to follow, and says so in a constant
.Dv FollowTinta :
the colours
.Pq Xr rat 1 , Xr pt 1 , Xr mosaico 1 ,
the mode only, with its own colours for each
.Pq Xr tiza 1 ,
or not at all.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn decode
returns nil, or
.Bl -tag -width BadMode -compact
.It Dv Short
fewer than
.Dv Items
items;
.It Dv Unknown
item 0 is not
.Dv Version ;
.It Dv BadMode
item 1 is neither 0 nor 1.
.El
.Pp
Each is a sentence for a program to print, as
.Fn MSG
gives it.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Reading the property with Xlib, whose items come as C longs:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
data: [Paleta.Items]U32;

pal := Paleta.Dark;
if XGetWindowProperty(dpy, root, atom, 0, Paleta.Items, 0,
    XA_CARDINAL, ADR(typ), ADR(fmt), ADR(n), ADR(after),
    ADR(got)) = Success then
  (* 8 bytes each: copy them into 4 *)
  for i := 0 to INT(n) - 1 do data[i] := U32(got[i * 8].(INT)); end;
  e := Paleta.decode(data[0..INT(n)], pal);
  XFree(got);
end;
.Ed
.Pp
Setting it, as
.Xr tinta 1
does:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
data := Paleta.encode(Paleta.Light);
(* copy into C longs, then XChangeProperty(..., 32, ...) *)
.Ed
.Pp
Using a slot:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
barBg := pal.col[Paleta.Bg];
urgent := pal.col[Paleta.Red];
dim := pal.col[Paleta.Black + Paleta.Bright];
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr tinta 1 ,
.Xr xprop 1 ,
.Xr desktop 7
.Sh STANDARDS
The contrast ratios are WCAG 2.1's, section 1.4.3 and 1.4.6.
The colours are Catppuccin's, version 1.
.Sh HISTORY
Written in 2026 with
.Xr tinta 1 ,
first as a Pascal unit and now as a raz module.
.Em Paleta
is palette, in Spanish.
.Xr rat 1
and
.Xr mosaico 1
import the installed copy rather than carry their own copies of
the decoding.
.Sh CAVEATS
Xlib's
.Fn XGetWindowProperty
returns format-32 data as C
.Vt long ,
eight bytes each on x86_64, not four.
Copy the items one by one into a
.Vt U32
array, as above; passing Xlib's buffer straight to
.Fn decode
reads every other item as zero, and fails with
.Dv Unknown
or, worse, not at all.
.Pp
A change of palette is one
.Dv PropertyNotify ;
a program that reads the property only at start never sees it.
